WEBVTT

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:14.666
Could this be...
could it be El Dorado?

00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:28.375
Oranges.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.583
Thousands of oranges.

00:00:30.708 --> 00:00:33.291
Juicy ones, at that!

00:00:33.416 --> 00:00:39.541
Oh... they're beautiful, perfect,
every one.

00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:42.125
But what about the gold?

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.708
I think we're looking at it.

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.833
The gold is oranges.

00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:53.166
El Dorado is an orange grove!

00:00:56.416 --> 00:00:58.958
- Oh...
- Oh! Oh!

00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:00.291
- Hello, again.
- Hello.

00:01:02.416 --> 00:01:04.833
- (Mr Brown) Oh?
- (Mrs Brown) Oh, look!

00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:06.541
(Growling)

00:01:07.541 --> 00:01:09.208
(Growling)

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:12.000
- (Mr Brown) And...?
- Hi!

00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:14.125
They're bears.

00:01:20.125 --> 00:01:21.625
(Gasps)

00:01:22.208 --> 00:01:24.375
(Roaring)

00:01:25.958 --> 00:01:27.458
- (Answering roar)
- Oh!

00:01:37.416 --> 00:01:39.416
Aunt Lucy!

00:01:40.291 --> 00:01:42.833
Ooh! Paddington?

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:50.958
Oh, Paddington!

00:01:52.791 --> 00:01:54.625
Aunt Lucy.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.208
I always knew you'd come for me.

00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:05.125
- You'll be needing these.
- Oh!

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.583
Oh... Oh, my, my...

00:02:08.708 --> 00:02:11.000
Quite the rescue party!

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:13.541
We're so pleased you're safe.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:16.291
All thanks to these bears.

00:02:17.708 --> 00:02:20.958
They heard my roars and rescued me.

00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:26.708
- Who are they, Aunt Lucy?
- And why do they dress up like... trees?

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:29.500
To stay hidden.

00:02:29.625 --> 00:02:34.791
They are the secret guardians
of the El Dorado oranges.

00:02:34.916 --> 00:02:37.250
They know my bear name.

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:41.750
And I've been dreaming
about that statue.

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:43.875
Help me up.

00:02:45.416 --> 00:02:51.541
All those years ago, when Uncle Pastuzo
and I found you as a cub,

00:02:51.666 --> 00:02:55.125
you told us you were orphaned.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.083
But we always wanted to know
where you came from.

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:05.000
The answer was here all the time.

00:03:05.125 --> 00:03:07.875
Your bracelet.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.833
All the cubs here have them.

00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:13.208
So, if they get lost...

00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:16.166
They can find their way home.

00:03:18.458 --> 00:03:20.791
(Aunt Lucy) They are your tribe.

00:03:22.125 --> 00:03:26.333
You are an El Dorado bear.

00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:30.791
I think... I remember.

00:03:36.708 --> 00:03:38.625
Uh... argh!

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.583
(Screaming)

00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:44.958
I got lost.

00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:50.416
In finding me,
you have found yourself.

00:04:01.083 --> 00:04:02.500
Oh...

00:04:05.625 --> 00:04:07.625
(Soft growling)

00:04:11.875 --> 00:04:14.666
What do you think they're saying?

00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:19.166
I think they're just...
happy to have him home.

00:04:22.791 --> 00:04:25.625
Would you mind taking these for me,
Mrs Brown?

00:04:26.416 --> 00:04:30.458
There's something I think
my tribe might enjoy.

00:04:30.583 --> 00:04:33.000
And I may need Jonathan's help.

00:04:59.625 --> 00:05:03.666
With oranges this wonderful,
you have to make marmalade.

00:05:05.291 --> 00:05:07.375
More sugar, please!

00:05:13.041 --> 00:05:15.041
He looks so happy.

00:05:16.166 --> 00:05:18.166
Like he never left.

00:05:18.291 --> 00:05:19.750
Now, Mary...

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.666
It's all right...
it's how it's meant to be.

00:05:27.041 --> 00:05:30.458
- Paddington belongs here.
- With his clan.

00:05:50.333 --> 00:05:53.583
(Paddington) 'Hello.
Coming down in stair rods, isn't it?'

00:05:54.166 --> 00:05:55.791
Good evening.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:04.458
(Mrs Brown) I hope you don't mind me asking,
but shouldn't you be at home?

00:06:04.583 --> 00:06:09.541
Oh, yes, I should, but I haven't quite
worked out how to find one.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.666
I didn't realise it was this difficult.

00:06:20.791 --> 00:06:22.208
Oh...

00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:28.333
It's just what happens next.

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:33.208
This one's for you.

00:06:35.791 --> 00:06:38.916
Thank you, Paddington. Gosh!

00:06:39.041 --> 00:06:42.291
No... thank you for...

00:06:43.375 --> 00:06:45.791
...well, for everything
you've done for me.

00:06:45.916 --> 00:06:47.583
And, um...

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:51.875
...there's something I wanted to ask.

00:06:52.541 --> 00:06:56.958
You were all so kind to take me in
all that time ago.

00:06:57.458 --> 00:06:59.041
I know it couldn't have been easy,

00:06:59.166 --> 00:07:01.666
and that I've sometimes been
a bit of a nuisance.

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:06.208
Sorry about that business with your pyjamas
in the paper shredder, Mr Brown.

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:09.000
That's all right, Paddington.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:15.166
So, I wanted to ask,
if you didn't mind terribly...

00:07:16.416 --> 00:07:19.250
...if, um...

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:25.916
...if I, um...

00:07:27.125 --> 00:07:28.625
Yes, Paddington?

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.916
Um...

00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:34.416
...if I could stay.

00:07:36.541 --> 00:07:37.791
Mmm...

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.666
With you.

00:07:40.791 --> 00:07:42.291
If I could come back home.

00:07:44.541 --> 00:07:47.208
Come back home with...? Oh!

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:51.458
Yes, of course you can, Paddington!
You don't have to ask that.

00:07:51.583 --> 00:07:55.458
Oh, I thought you were going
to ask to stay here.

00:07:55.583 --> 00:07:59.833
Mrs Brown... they are my tribe.

00:08:00.625 --> 00:08:02.583
But you are my family.

00:08:02.708 --> 00:08:07.625
This is where I'm from,
but... you're where I belong.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:10.166
- Oh.
- (Chuckling)

